# Flow Choices

A Choice routes one Flow node to one Action or nested Flow. It checks options in
authored order, runs the first matching target, and uses a required
`otherwise` target when no option matches.

A Choice selects a target before that target runs. Its fallback is routing
logic, not error recovery.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0"}])
```

## Define A Map Action

```elixir
defmodule FlowChoices.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_choices_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Define A Choice

Every `option` has a unique name, a `when:` condition, a `run:` target, and a
`with:` target input. `otherwise` has a `run:` target and a `with:` input.

```elixir
defmodule FlowChoices.Routing do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_choices_routing",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{tier: Zoi.atom()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    priority_data = step(FlowChoices.Echo, with: %{kind: value(:priority)})
    bulk_data = step(FlowChoices.Echo, with: %{kind: value(:bulk)})
    fallback_data = step(FlowChoices.Echo, with: %{kind: value(:standard)})

    selected_route =
      choose :route do
        option(:priority,
          when:
            all([
              eq(input(:tier), value(:priority)),
              eq(result(:priority_data, :kind), value(:priority))
            ]),
          run: FlowChoices.Echo,
          with: %{route: value(:priority)}
        )

        option(:bulk,
          when:
            all([
              eq(input(:tier), value(:bulk)),
              eq(result(:bulk_data, :kind), value(:bulk))
            ]),
          run: FlowChoices.Echo,
          with: %{route: value(:bulk)}
        )

        otherwise(
          run: FlowChoices.Echo,
          with: %{route: select(fallback_data, :kind)}
        )
      end

    return(selected_route)
  end
end
```

The result references in conditions and the fallback input create static
dependencies. All three predecessor nodes run before `route` is ready, even
though only one target runs.

```elixir
{:ok, dependencies} = Jido.Flow.dependencies(FlowChoices.Routing.flow())

["bulk_data", "fallback_data", "priority_data"] = dependencies["route"]
```

## Ordered First Match And Short-Circuiting

Jido tests options in authored order. The first true condition wins. Later
conditions are not evaluated, and only the selected target runs. `all` stops at
the first false child. `any` stops at the first true child.

```elixir
{:ok, %{route: :priority}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :priority}, %{})

{:ok, %{route: :bulk}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :bulk}, %{})

{:ok, %{route: :standard}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :other}, %{})
```

## Condition Primitives

Choice conditions are closed data. The supported primitives are:

- `eq(left, right)` and `neq(left, right)` compare values.
- `lt`, `lte`, `gt`, and `gte` compare two numbers or two strings.
- `left in right` checks membership in a proper list.
- `all([conditions])` requires every child condition.
- `any([conditions])` requires one child condition.
- `not(condition)` inverts one condition.

You can construct each primitive directly through `Jido.Flow.Syntax`:

```elixir
alias Jido.Flow.Syntax

conditions = [
  Syntax.eq(Syntax.input(:kind), Syntax.value(:priority)),
  Syntax.neq(Syntax.input(:kind), Syntax.value(:blocked)),
  Syntax.lt(Syntax.input(:rank), Syntax.value(10)),
  Syntax.lte(Syntax.input(:rank), Syntax.value(10)),
  Syntax.gt(Syntax.input(:rank), Syntax.value(1)),
  Syntax.gte(Syntax.input(:rank), Syntax.value(1)),
  apply(Syntax, :in, [Syntax.input(:kind), Syntax.value([:priority, :bulk])]),
  Syntax.all([Syntax.eq(Syntax.value(true), Syntax.value(true))]),
  Syntax.any([Syntax.eq(Syntax.value(true), Syntax.value(false))]),
  Syntax.not(Syntax.eq(Syntax.value(true), Syntax.value(false)))
]

10 = length(conditions)
```

Conditions can use the normal data references: `input(path)`,
`context(path)`, `value(term)`, `result(node, path)`, and
`select(source, path)`. A missing path resolves to `nil`.

## `otherwise` Is Required

A Choice must contain at least one option and exactly one fallback. The parser
rejects a Choice without `otherwise`.

```elixir
{:error, missing_fallback} =
  Jido.Flow.parse(
    """
    flow do
      selected = choose :route do
        option(:yes,
          when: eq(input(:kind), value(:yes)),
          run: FlowChoices.Echo,
          with: %{route: value(:yes)}
        )
      end

      return(selected)
    end
    """,
    name: "missing_fallback"
  )

true = Exception.message(missing_fallback) =~ "choice"
```

## Fallback Is Not Error Recovery

The fallback is selected only when no option condition is true. It does not run
when a selected target returns an error.

```elixir
defmodule FlowChoices.Failing do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_choices_failing",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(_params, _context), do: {:error, :selected_target_failed}
end

defmodule FlowChoices.NoRecovery do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_choices_no_recovery",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    selected_route =
      choose :route do
        option(:selected,
          when: eq(value(true), value(true)),
          run: FlowChoices.Failing,
          with: %{}
        )

        otherwise(run: FlowChoices.Echo, with: %{route: value(:fallback)})
      end

    return(selected_route)
  end
end

{:error, _error} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.NoRecovery, %{}, %{})
```

## Build A Choice With `Jido.Flow.Builder`

Use the Builder when application code must assemble Flow syntax at runtime.
It lowers to the same canonical Flow artifact as the module DSL.

```elixir
alias Jido.Flow.Builder

builder =
  Builder.new(name: "builder_choice")
  |> Builder.choice(
    :route,
    [
      Builder.option(
        :yes,
        Builder.eq(Builder.input(:kind), Builder.value(:yes)),
        FlowChoices.Echo,
        %{route: Builder.value(:yes)}
      )
    ],
    Builder.fallback(FlowChoices.Echo, %{route: Builder.value(:no)}),
    bind: :selected
  )
  |> Builder.return(Builder.binding(:selected))

{:ok, built_flow} = Builder.build(builder)
%Jido.Flow{name: "builder_choice"} = built_flow
```

## Step Through A Choice

The public step-wise API exposes the Choice as one node. It does not expose
each option or the selected target as a parent node.

```elixir
{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.start(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :priority}, %{})

{:ok, _predecessors, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)
["route"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok, %Jido.Exec.NodeResult{node: "route", status: :ok, output: %{route: :priority}}, execution} =
  Jido.Exec.step(execution, "route")

{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.continue(execution)
{:ok, %{route: :priority}} = Jido.Exec.result(execution)
```

